1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,970
It is July eighteen ninety-seven.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:06,770
You are twenty-two years old, standing on
the crowded docks of Seattle.

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,330
A steamship named the
Portland just tied up,

4
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:15,030
carrying sixty-eight miners and more
than a ton of pure gold.

5
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:19,730
That young fool staring at the heavy
leather sacks was me.

6
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,910
My name is Walt Becker.

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,930
Tonight, I will tell you how we
chased a dream into the

8
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:26,630
frozen North.

9
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,810
The city went mad overnight.

10
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:32,570
Clerks quit their jobs, doctors
left their clinics,

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:36,230
and shopkeepers sold everything to
buy a ticket North.

12
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:40,070
I had three hundred dollars
saved from farm work.

13
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:44,570
I bought passage on a rusted
steamer bound for Dyea,

14
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:44,830
Alaska.

15
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:48,890
The papers called it the last great
adventure of the century.

16
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,370
They promised gold lying in creek
beds like yellow gravel.

17
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:56,830
On the crowded deck, I met Sam Miller,

18
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,650
a blacksmith from Ohio.

19
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,610
We agreed to pool our money and
travel as partners.

20
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,450
Sam was strong as an ox and
always had a warm smile.

21
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,470
We thought we would be rich
before Christmas.

22
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:15,070
When our ship pulled into the
muddy inlet of Dyea,

23
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:18,470
Alaska, reality hit us like a
bucket of ice water.

24
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:25,410
There was no town, no docks,

25
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:25,490
and no hotel.

26
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,210
Just thousands of desperate men thrown
onto a stony beach in the

27
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:29,010
freezing rain.

28
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:31,950
We pitched a crude tent in the mud,

29
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,950
shivering as cold ocean wind
whipped off the inlet waves.

30
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:40,450
We had no idea that the real test
had not even begun.

31
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:44,990
By autumn, winter was already creeping
down from the mountain peaks,

32
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:47,390
coating the valley in gray slush.

33
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:53,310
At the border, the Canadian North-West
Mounted Police posted a strict legal

34
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:56,130
warning for every stampeder.

35
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:59,370
Inspector Charles Constantine
made the law clear.

36
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:04,910
No man could enter Canada without two
thousand pounds of food and

37
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:05,730
survival gear.

38
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:08,150
Two thousand pounds.

39
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:11,450
One whole ton of supplies per person,

40
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,030
meant to keep a miner alive for
one full year in the

41
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:15,270
North.

42
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,550
The mandatory list required five
hundred pounds of flour,

43
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:23,250
one hundred pounds of bacon, dried beans,

44
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:26,530
coffee, blankets, and hand tools.

45
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,710
Sam and I spent nearly all our
remaining cash buying sacks of

46
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:35,370
flour and salted pork at
outrageous frontier prices.

47
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:42,998
We had no pack horses.

48
00:02:42,848 --> 00:02:43,070
Every single pound had to be
carried on our own backs,

49
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,150
forty or fifty pounds at a time.

50
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:48,370
The trail from Dyea to the base of
the pass was twenty-six

51
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:51,030
miles of thick mud, tree roots,

52
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:52,350
and icy streams.

53
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,970
We hauled fifty pounds five miles up,

54
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:57,710
dropped the load on the snow,

55
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:00,810
and walked five miles back for
the next bundle.

56
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,310
To move two tons of gear,

57
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:07,390
Sam and I had to walk that muddy
mountain trail back and

58
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:08,770
forth forty times.

59
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:11,610
Before we even saw a mountain peak,

60
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,710
we had walked over one thousand
miles carrying heavy packs.

61
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:19,510
At night, we slept under wet canvas tents,

62
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:24,290
shivering as the freezing rain turned
into thick winter snow.

63
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:29,150
Sam developed a deep, rattling cough
that never quite went away,

64
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,770
but he refused to stop hauling.

65
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:36,690
By November, the trail disappeared
under five feet of fresh snow.

66
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:39,330
We reached the foot of Chilkoot Pass.

67
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:42,410
Looking up at that sheer
wall of white ice,

68
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,390
my heart dropped straight into
my leather boots.

69
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:49,190
And the worst part of the mountain
was still waiting for us.

70
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:54,130
Chilkoot Pass rose thirty-five hundred
feet into freezing clouds,

71
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,250
a steep wall of solid snow and ice.

72
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:02,570
Miners had carved fifteen hundred steps
straight up the face of the

73
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:03,130
snow slope.

74
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,990
We called them the Golden Stairs.

75
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:09,190
The slope was forty-five degrees steep.

76
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:12,930
You had to kick your heavy leather
boots into the step ahead

77
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:13,310
of you.

78
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,670
Thousands of men climbed in single file,

79
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:21,570
locked shoulder to shoulder like an
endless black snake on the white

80
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:21,870
ice.

81
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,490
If you stepped out of line to rest,

82
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:28,850
you might wait two hours before anyone
would let you back into

83
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:29,230
line.

84
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:34,990
Each trip up the Golden Stairs took
six agonizing hours under a

85
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:36,470
heavy fifty-pound pack.

86
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,590
Let me ask you something.

87
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:42,670
If you had to climb an icy mountain
forty times with heavy

88
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:45,670
bags, would you turn back or press on?

89
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:47,570
Tell me in the comments.

90
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:52,750
Indigenous Tlingit packers helped carry
loads for those who could pay,

91
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:55,330
charging up to fifty cents a pound.

92
00:04:55,180 --> 00:05:00,130
Fifty cents a pound was a fortune in
eighteen ninety-seven,

93
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:02,670
but many men had no strength left to haul.

94
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,590
The weather on the pass was merciless.

95
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:11,350
Sudden blizzards dropped temperatures to
forty below zero within minutes.

96
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:15,270
One morning, an avalanche swept
down the mountain,

97
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:18,490
burying sixty men under deep snow.

98
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,470
Sam and I dug with our bare
hands for three hours,

99
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:26,590
pulling two men out alive before
frost bit our fingers.

100
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:31,330
By mid-December, after three brutal
months of constant climbing,

101
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:35,190
our entire ton of gear was
finally atop the pass.

102
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:37,990
We stood at the Canadian border post,

103
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:41,030
exhausted, battered, but victorious.

104
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,270
The Mountie inspector checked
off our supplies,

105
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:48,390
stamped our papers, and welcomed us
into the Yukon territory.

106
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:52,870
We sledded our two thousand pounds of
gear down to Lake Bennett

107
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,610
and set up winter camp.

108
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,890
Now we had to wait six long
winter months for the ice

109
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:00,030
on the river to thaw.

110
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:02,570
Winter at Lake Bennett was quiet,

111
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:04,810
cold, and deadly.

112
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:08,490
Thirty thousand people were camped
around Lake Bennett,

113
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:11,730
waiting for spring to melt the
thick lake ice.

114
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:15,730
A massive tent city had sprung up
on the frozen shore.

115
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:19,390
It was the largest town of
canvas in the world.

116
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:24,430
To travel five hundred miles down the
Yukon River to the goldfields,

117
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:26,110
every team needed a wooden boat.

118
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:27,890
There were no sawmills.

119
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:32,070
We had to chop down massive pine
trees and haul the heavy

120
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:33,370
logs on sleds.

121
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:36,970
We dug a saw pit six feet into
the frozen earth.

122
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:40,450
Sam stood down in the pit
while I stood above.

123
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:45,850
For weeks, sawdust poured down into
Sam's face as we pulled a

124
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:48,030
six-foot hand saw up and down.

125
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:51,710
We cut hundreds of feet of
green pine lumber,

126
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,110
our hands blistered and bleeding
from the raw work.

127
00:06:55,860 --> 00:07:00,230
We sealed the wooden seams with hot
pitch and frayed rope to

128
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:02,050
make our scow boat watertight.

129
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:05,410
We named our boat the Northern Star.

130
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,270
She was ugly and heavy, but
she was strong.

131
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:13,650
Sam painted our boat's name on the
bow in dark red paint.

132
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,670
For a moment, we felt like true pioneers.

133
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:23,230
On May twenty-ninth, eighteen ninety-eight, the
ice on Lake Bennett suddenly cracked

134
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:25,230
with a roar like cannon fire.

135
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:31,030
Within hours, seven thousand handmade boats
pushed off into the cold water

136
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:33,130
at the exact same time.

137
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:37,130
It was an astonishing armada
of wooden rafts,

138
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:41,010
skiffs, and canoes drifting down
the clear blue lakes.

139
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,250
The water carried us northward
toward the Klondike River.

140
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,510
Gold was only two hundred miles away.

141
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:53,050
We thought the worst of our
hardships were behind us as the

142
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:54,530
current pulled our boat along.

143
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:58,270
Then we heard the roaring
thunder of Miles Canyon.

144
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:01,710
The Yukon River was about to
claim its price.

145
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:07,170
Miles Canyon was a narrow basalt gorge
where the roaring river squeezed

146
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:09,250
into a raging white torrent.

147
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:16,350
Swirling whirlpools and jagged black rocks
swallowed wooden boats whole.

148
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:18,610
Dozens of men had already drowned.

149
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:24,650
Inspector Jack Norwood of the Mounties
halted all unguided boats at the

150
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:25,990
canyon entrance for safety.

151
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:32,590
Experienced boat pilots charged twenty-five
dollars to steer rafts through the deadly

152
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:33,790
Whitehorse Rapids.

153
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:37,310
Sam and I did not have
twenty-five dollars left.

154
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,830
We had to pilot our own heavy wooden scow.

155
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:45,030
Sam took the bow sweep and I
grabbed the stern oar.

156
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:47,990
We plunged into the roaring white foam.

157
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:52,570
The wall of water threw our heavy
boat like a pine toothpick.

158
00:08:53,220 --> 00:08:55,590
Water surged over the wooden sides!

159
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:58,210
Sam yelled at the top of his lungs,

160
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:02,290
pulling his oar with every ounce of
strength he had left.

161
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:07,670
We shot through the roaring canyon in
three terrifying minutes and spun

162
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,210
out into calm water below.

163
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:13,850
We were soaked to the skin and half
our flour sacks were

164
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:15,750
ruined, but we were alive.

165
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:20,270
We bailed out the boat, dried our
gear in the spring sun,

166
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:22,550
and drifted the final miles.

167
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:28,650
On June eighth, eighteen ninety-eight, our
boat rounded the river bend and

168
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:30,010
Dawson City appeared.

169
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:33,810
What we found in Dawson was not
what any of us had

170
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:34,070
dreamed.

171
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:38,530
Dawson City was a booming town
built on a muddy swamp,

172
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:41,770
packed with forty thousand
desperate souls.

173
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:48,270
Log cabins, dance halls, and muddy streets
were jammed with stampeders who

174
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:49,450
had arrived too late.

175
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:54,690
Every single gold claim on Bonanza Creek
had been staked two years

176
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,890
earlier, in eighteen ninety-six.

177
00:09:57,740 --> 00:09:59,790
Prices in town were insane.

178
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:03,210
Fresh eggs cost three dollars each,

179
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:07,740
and a single loaf of bread was one dollar.

180
00:10:07,590 --> 00:10:11,510
Men who had carried a ton of supplies
across mountains were selling

181
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,360
their gear just to buy a
steamboat ticket home.

182
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:17,620
Scurvy broke out in the overcrowded camps.

183
00:10:18,370 --> 00:10:22,440
Sam's gums bled and his legs
grew swollen and dark.

184
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:26,820
I spent our last silver coins on a
jar of spruce-needle tea

185
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:30,040
and raw potatoes to save Sam's life.

186
00:10:30,790 --> 00:10:34,700
Out of one hundred thousand men who
set out for the Klondike,

187
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:37,760
only four thousand ever found gold.

188
00:10:38,330 --> 00:10:40,520
Sam and I never found gold.

189
00:10:40,930 --> 00:10:45,000
In August, we worked as deckhands on
a riverboat to pay for

190
00:10:44,850 --> 00:10:45,820
our passage south.

191
00:10:46,650 --> 00:10:48,340
If you have stayed with me this far,

192
00:10:48,790 --> 00:10:53,320
leave a like and subscribe; that
is how I know you want

193
00:10:53,170 --> 00:10:54,960
more stories like this one.

194
00:10:55,590 --> 00:10:59,320
We returned home to Ohio without a
single ounce of gold,

195
00:10:59,810 --> 00:11:02,560
but with something far more precious.

196
00:11:03,470 --> 00:11:06,580
We survived the greatest winter
trek in human history,

197
00:11:07,230 --> 00:11:10,280
shoulder to shoulder with men
who never quit.

198
00:11:11,110 --> 00:11:13,580
That gold rush made boys into men,

199
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:16,180
and made brothers out of strangers.

200
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:19,260
The gold in the ground stays frozen,

201
00:11:20,370 --> 00:11:24,700
but the warmth of those old memories
keeps me going by this

202
00:11:24,550 --> 00:11:25,020
stove.

203
00:11:24,870 --> 00:11:27,420
Goodnight, my friends.
